### Secrets kept out of state

With Terraform 1.11 or later, `content_wo` is a [write-only argument](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/resources/ephemeral#write-only-arguments): its value is sent to the provider when the file is written, but it is never stored in the plan or the state. It can therefore come from an [ephemeral variable](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/values/variables#exclude-values-from-state) or an [ephemeral resource](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/resources/ephemeral), which Terraform rejects in `content` and `sensitive_content`:

{{ tffile "examples/resources/sysutils_file/write_only.tf" }}

Because Terraform keeps no copy of the value, it can't tell when the value changes. The file is written:

- when the resource is created;
- when `content_wo_version` changes (including being set or removed). Increase it whenever you want the current value of `content_wo` written, for example together with a secret rotation;
- when the file on disk no longer matches what the provider last wrote (see [Drift Detection](#drift-detection));
- on the first apply after switching from another content attribute, or after import.

A changed `content_wo` alone plans no change.

`content_sha256` and `content_md5` are always null with `content_wo`: a checksum of a short secret, such as a password, would let anyone who can read the state check guesses at high speed. When an existing file will be rewritten, the plan shows them as `(known after apply)`, and they become null again. To make other resources react to a new value, for example `restart_on_change` of `sysutils_service`, use `content_wo_version`; a file rewritten only because of drift restores the content the service already had.

With Terraform before 1.11 and OpenTofu before 1.11, a configuration that sets `content_wo` fails validation with "Write-only attributes are only supported in Terraform 1.11 and later".

The provider's own ephemeral resources are made for this. The [`sysutils_file` ephemeral resource](../ephemeral-resources/file.md) reads a secret from a local file, and refuses files and directories that anyone but root or the user running Terraform could change, so that nobody can slip in a value of their own. The [`sysutils_exec` ephemeral resource](../ephemeral-resources/exec.md) runs a command, for example a vault or password manager CLI, and returns its output. Neither stores its configuration or its results anywhere:

{{ tffile "examples/ephemeral-resources/sysutils_exec/ephemeral-resource.tf" }}

`sysutils_template_file` has no write-only variant, because it records the rendered content in state for its diffs. To render a template with secrets that must not be stored, pass `templatefile()` to `content_wo`; its result is ephemeral if any of its variables is.

